  "descriptionKey": "- Official development assistance (ODA) is aid given to countries and territories on the OECD Development Assistance Committee (DAC) list of recipients and multilateral development institutions. To qualify as ODA, the aid has to serve the economic development and welfare of recipient countries and be either a grant or a loan with favorable terms.\n- Donors mark their bilateral aid projects with the \"Rio markers\", which track support for the three UN conventions from the 1992 Rio Earth Summit: on climate change (with separate markers for mitigation and adaptation), biodiversity, and desertification. For each marker, a goal is a \"principal\" objective (the project wouldn't have been funded without it), a \"significant\" objective (an explicit but secondary one), or not targeted.\n- This data covers bilateral allocable ODA from [DAC members](https://www.oecd.org/en/about/committees/development-assistance-committee.html), the major donor countries in the OECD and EU institutions. This is the bilateral aid that the Rio markers apply to. It excludes general budget support, debt relief (other than debt swaps), administrative costs, development awareness, and the costs of hosting refugees and students from developing countries in donor countries. It also excludes core contributions to multilateral organizations, such as the World Bank, and aid from donors outside the DAC. We also leave out the European Investment Bank, which doesn't use the Rio markers.\n- Climate change mitigation projects aim to reduce greenhouse gas emissions or increase their removal by carbon sinks. Climate change adaptation projects aim to reduce the vulnerability of people and ecosystems to the impacts of climate change.\n- Many projects target both mitigation and adaptation, so we count each project once: as mitigation only, adaptation only, or both. Climate is a project's principal objective if either marker is principal, and a significant objective otherwise.\n- Projects count at their full value even when climate is only a significant objective, so the OECD calls this data descriptive rather than strictly quantitative. Donors mark their own projects, so for some it's hard to tell whether climate change was the reason for the aid, or just a label on a project that would have gone ahead anyway.\n- The data shows commitments: aid pledged, rather than aid disbursed. The OECD applies the Rio markers to commitments because they describe projects as designed. A multi-year project counts in full in the year it is committed, so values can change a lot from year to year.\n- The adaptation marker was introduced in 2009 and applies to aid from 2010, when this data starts. Before then, the climate marker also covered some adaptation projects, so climate-related aid can't be split into mitigation and adaptation.\n- Projects that donors did not assess against the climate markers (\"not screened\") count as non-climate aid. This was common in the early 2010s and still is for some donors. Donors that leave only their non-climate projects unscreened are unaffected; donors that leave almost all their projects unscreened can show little or no climate-related aid even if they gave some.\n- Entities named \"regional (OECD)\" or \"unspecified (OECD)\", such as \"Africa, regional (OECD)\", show aid that donors did not assign to a single country, for example programs covering several countries. This aid is included in the totals of the corresponding regions, such as Africa (OECD).",
